@*
    The Explorer's routable shell surface, and the one place the shell's route
    model is bound to the browser.

    Every template but the bare "/" begins with a LITERAL segment, and that is
    load-bearing rather than stylistic. The Explorer is an embeddable library
    mounted under a caller-chosen prefix, sharing that mount with framework and
    static assets (_framework/**, _content/**, published files). A template whose
    first segment is a parameter - and a root catch-all most of all - matches
    those too, so an asset request renders the whole admin console at an asset URL
    and comes back carrying two Content-Security-Policy headers, which browsers
    resolve as the intersection of both policies. Literal-first makes that
    impossible by construction instead of relying on route precedence, and it
    survives being rebased under a base path, which a root catch-all does not.

    The home area owns the literal 'explore'. A contributed area's slug is only
    known at run time, so it cannot own a literal and is namespaced under '/area/'
    instead - one extra segment, in exchange for no contributed slug ever being
    able to collide with an asset path.

    The route parameters are declared because the templates name them, but they
    are not read. The address is parsed from NavigationManager.Uri by
    ExplorerRoutePath, because a selection id is cluster-owned and may contain a
    slash (a tenant-owned tree is t/acme/orders); escaping it and parsing it here
    is what keeps such an id addressable at all.

    The markup is unchanged: the chrome around it belongs to the layout and the
    app shell, and this page contributes only the detail surface it always did.
*@

<PageTitle>@ExplorerInfo.DisplayName</PageTitle>

<DetailPanel />
